= Language — Simple =

'''Language''' is a structured way people communicate and think with signs.

A language can be spoken, signed, and sometimes written. Signed languages are full languages, so language is not the same thing as speech.

Languages have several kinds of structure. Words and signs can be combined using [[Syntax/Simple|syntax]]. They carry meaning, studied in [[Semantics/Simple|semantics]]. People also use context, shared knowledge, and intentions to understand what someone means; that is part of [[Pragmatics/Simple|pragmatics]].

People can understand sentences they have never heard before because language is productive and systematic. But language is not perfectly mechanical. Words can be ambiguous, have several related meanings, or mean something different in different situations.

Linguists study how languages are structured, learned, used, changed, and processed.

A natural human language is different from a programming language. Programming languages are deliberately defined formal systems; human languages grow and change through communities and cognition.

